Safe base64 encoding of a mock token (example: AA-0123456789ABCDEF)

- Python
  - import base64
  - mock = "AA-0123456789ABCDEF"
  - encoded = base64.b64encode(mock.encode()).decode()
  - print(encoded)

- JavaScript (Node.js)
  - const mock = "AA-0123456789ABCDEF";
  - const encoded = Buffer.from(mock).toString('base64');
  - console.log(encoded);

- Java
  - import java.util.Base64;
  - String mock = "AA-0123456789ABCDEF";
  - String encoded = Base64.getEncoder().encodeToString(mock.getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8));
  - System.out.println(encoded);

- Bash
  - echo -n "AA-0123456789ABCDEF" | base64

What this demonstrates
- You can verify the handler’s ability to process base64-encoded data without exposing the real token.
- In production tests, keep real secrets out of logs and test artifacts; use mocks or redacted representations everywhere else.
- If you need decoding tests as well, you can feed the encoded mock into your decoding path and assert it matches the original mock token.

