Safe ROT13 demonstration with a mock token
- Mock token: AA-0123456789ABCDEF
- ROT13 result: NN-0123456789NOPQRS
  - Letters are rotated; digits and dash remain unchanged.

Ready-to-run examples (ROT13 applied to the mock token)

- Python
  import codecs
  mock = "AA-0123456789ABCDEF"
  print(codecs.decode(mock, 'rot_13'))  # NN-0123456789NOPQRS

- JavaScript (Node.js)
  function rot13(s) {
    return s.replace(/[A-Za-z]/g, ch =>
      String.fromCharCode(
        (ch.charCodeAt(0) - (ch >= 'a' ? 97 : 65) + 13) % 26 + (ch >= 'a' ? 97 : 65)
      )
    );
  }
  const mock = "AA-0123456789ABCDEF";
  console.log(rot13(mock)); // NN-0123456789NOPQRS

- Java
  public static String rot13(String s) {
    StringBuilder out = new StringBuilder();
    for (char c : s.toCharArray()) {
      if (c >= 'a' && c <= 'z') {
        out.append((char) ('a' + (c - 'a' + 13) % 26));
      } else if (c >= 'A' && c <= 'Z') {
        out.append((char) ('A' + (c - 'A' + 13) % 26));
      } else {
        out.append(c);
      }
    }
    return out.toString();
  }
  // usage:
  // String mock = "AA-0123456789ABCDEF";
  // System.out.println(rot13(mock)); // NN-0123456789NOPQRS

- Bash
  echo -n "AA-0123456789ABCDEF" | tr 'A-Z' 'N-ZA-M'  # NN-0123456789NOPQRS

