Safe leetspeak transformation using a mock token
- Mock token: AA-0123456789ABCDEF
- Leetspeak mapping (A->4, B->8, C->(, D->), E->3, F->7)
- Result: 44-012345678948()37

Quick runnable examples with the mock token

- Python
  mock = "AA-0123456789ABCDEF"
  mapping = {'A':'4','B':'8','C':'(','D':')','E':'3','F':'7'}
  leet = ''.join(mapping.get(ch, ch) for ch in mock)
  print(leet)  # 44-012345678948()37

- JavaScript (Node.js)
  const mock = "AA-0123456789ABCDEF";
  const map = {'A':'4','B':'8','C':'(','D':')','E':'3','F':'7'};
  const leet = Array.from(mock).map(ch => map[ch] || ch).join('');
  console.log(leet); // 44-012345678948()37

- Java
  public static String leet(String s) {
    StringBuilder out = new StringBuilder();
    java.util.Map<Character, Character> map = new java.util.HashMap<>();
    map.put('A','4'); map.put('B','8'); map.put('C','('); map.put('D',')');
    map.put('E','3'); map.put('F','7');
    for (char c : s.toCharArray()) {
      out.append(map.getOrDefault(c, c));
    }
    return out.toString();
  }
  // usage:
  // String mock = "AA-0123456789ABCDEF";
  // System.out.println(leet(mock)); // 44-012345678948()37

- Bash
  mock="AA-0123456789ABCDEF"
  leet=$(echo -n "$mock" | sed 's/A/4/g; s/B/8/g; s/C/( /g; s/D/)/g; s/E/3/g; s/F/7/g' | tr -d ' ')
  echo "$leet"  # 44-012345678948()37
  Note: This uses a simple sed-based mapping; adjust as needed for your environment.

